Which socket does ph-cli talk to?

ph-cli needs no sudo. By default it searches for the packet handler's
control socket in this order (zipline#39):

1. The per-user socket for your uid: /var/run/zpr/<uid>/control.sock.
   This is where a sudo- or pkexec-started ph puts it, owned by the user
   who ran sudo, mode 0600. The base /var/run/zpr is fixed (never derived
   from HOME/XDG_DATA_HOME) so ph and ph-cli agree across the sudo
   privilege boundary.
2. The shared socket: <data_home>/zpr/control.sock. This is where a
   systemd-started ph puts it; if the host has a "zpr" group the socket is
   group zpr, mode 0660, so group members can use ph-cli directly.

If neither answers, ph-cli fails with an error naming both paths it tried.
A candidate is selected by probe-connecting to it, not by checking the
pathname exists, so a stale socket file left by a dead ph never shadows a
live server at the other path.
An explicit -p (control socket) or -c (capture socket) always wins and
short-circuits the search. The default capture socket is capture.sock in
the same directory as the control socket that was found.

Security note: reaching the control socket means being able to start and
stop links and register an AuthAgent — i.e. to supply and observe user
credentials for this adapter, and to receive the IdP client_secret in a
HelloResponse. Socket access is adapter control; the zpr group is not a
low-privilege convenience.

How to generate Bash completion script 

Compile the program using cargo build

Run the program using the generate flag and the path to the file:

./target/debug/ph-cli --generate <FILE_PATH>

Run 'source ./FILE_PATH' 
This will only work for your session,if you want it to be permanent, you can 
add it to /etc/bash_completion.d or put source ./ph-cli.bash in your 
.bashrc or .bash_profile

Now run the program:

target/debug/ph-cli

And you can tab complete command line arguments 
